Use Case:-

Here we have used api gateway and http route for routing North - South traffic.
Service Routers used for routing rules between internal service mesh East - West traffic.
Below is the expected flow:-
######## North - South ###########
Note localhost:32338 is the api gateway url.
curl localhost:32338    -> static-server
curl localhost:32338    -> static-server (If not available) -> failover to non-mesh service static-server-nonmesh 
curl localhost:32338/tea -> static-server-tea-v1 (10% of traffic), static-server-tea-v2 (90% of traffic) -> If any of v1, v2 or both not available then failover to v1, v2 services in backup namespace
curl localhost:32338/coffee -> static-server-coffee -> If not available -> failover to static-server-coffee in backup namespace
curl -s -H "x-debug: 1"  localhost:32338 -> static-server-header-query-param -> If not available -> failover to static-server-header-query-param in backup namespace [But the faiover will not work here because no Meshservice created for this service]
curl -s "http://localhost:32338?x-debug=1" -> static-server-header-query-param -> If not available -> failover to static-server-header-query-param in backup namespace [But the faiover will not work here because no Meshservice created for this service]
########## East - West Flow ############
When we login to the static-client app in service mesh with consul
curl static-server.virtual.consul   -> static-server (If not available) -> failover to non-mesh service static-server-nonmesh 
curl static-server.virtual.consul/tea -> static-server-tea-v1 (10% of traffic), static-server-tea-v2 (90% of traffic) -> If any of v1, v2 or both not available then failover to v1, v2 services in backup namespace
curl static-server.virtual.consul/coffee -> curl localhost:32338/coffee -> static-server-coffee -> If not available -> failover to static-server-coffee in backup namespace
curl -s -H "x-debug: 1"  static-server.virtual.consul -> static-server-header-query-param -> If not available -> failover to static-server-header-query-param in backup namespace [failover will work here unlike N-S traffic bcos it is E-W traffic]
curl "static-server.virtual.consul/coffee?x-debug=1" -> static-server-header-query-param -> If not available -> failover to static-server-header-query-param in backup namespace [failover will work here unlike N-S traffic bcos it is E-W traffic]

Note:-
Do not get confused with routing logic defined in http routes of api gw and service router. 
Service router routing logic is for service mesh services only i.e for East - West traffic.
While routing logic defined in http route is for North - South traffic.
If you need to apply Consul service splitter and resolver logic to the service mesh services defined in http routes, you NEED TO CREATE Meshservice for them and define in http route config.
You cannot access non-mesh services through api gateway, to use them you have to use terminating gateway.
I have not used ACLs here.

Deployment steps:-
kind create cluster --config=kind.yaml
secret=$(cat license.hclic)
kubectl create secret generic consul-ent-license --from-literal="key=${secret}"
helm install consul hashicorp/consul --values=values.yaml --version=1.8.15 --debug
kubectl apply -f service-router.yaml
kubectl apply -f service-splitter.yaml
kubectl apply -f service-router.yaml
kubectl apply -f proxy-defaults.yaml
cd default_ns_apps
./apply-all.sh
cd ../backup_ns_apps
kubectl create ns backup
./apply-all.sh
cd ..
kubectl apply -f meshservice-static-server.yaml
kubectl apply -f meshservice-static-server-tea.yaml
kubectl apply -f meshservice-static-server-coffee.yaml
kubectl apply -f meshservice-static-server-nonmesh.yaml
kubectl port-forward svc/consul-server 8501:8501    (on other terminal)
export CONSUL_HTTP_ADDR=https://127.0.0.1:8501
export CONSUL_HTTP_SSL_VERIFY=false
To register external service static-server-nonmesh into Consul, run below command:-
curl -k --request PUT --data @ext.json https://localhost:8501/v1/catalog/register
kubectl apply -f terminating-gw.yaml
kubectl create ns consul
kubectl apply -f api-gw.yaml
kubectl apply -f http-route.yaml


####################################### WITH ACLS ################ 
Steps:
Add below block to values.yaml :-

acls:
    manageSystemACLs: true

and perform helm upgrade: 
helm upgrade consul hashicorp/consul --values=values.yaml --version=1.8.15 --debug

Apply service intentions:-
kubectl apply -f service-intentions.yaml

Validate again
###############################

